<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="UTF-8"/><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width"/><meta name="description" content="Campagnolo Valentino: the affordable range that brought the Campagnolo name to ordinary sports bicycles."/><title>Campagnolo Valentino · The Evolution of Bikes</title><link rel="stylesheet" href={`${base}/styles/global.css`}/></head><body><EnglishHeader current="components" danishPath="/komponenter/campagnolo-valentino/"/><main><article class="component-page">
<header class="component-hero"><div><p class="section-label">Component archive · 1967–1980s</p><h1>Campagnolo<br/><em>Valentino</em></h1></div><p>When Campagnolo built for riders beyond the elite.</p></header><figure class="component-hero-image component-photo"><img src={`${images}/bagskifter.jpg`} alt="Campagnolo Valentino Extra rear derailleur"/><figcaption>A Valentino Extra on a bicycle in the collection. Photo: The Evolution of Bikes.</figcaption></figure>
<section class="component-opening"><p class="section-label">Another Campagnolo</p><div><p>Valentino was not Record in cheap disguise. It was Campagnolo’s attempt to serve sports bicycles where price, simplicity and a familiar name mattered more than professional prestige.</p></div></section>
<section class="component-facts" aria-label="Campagnolo Valentino facts">{facts.map(([l,v])=><div><dt>{l}</dt><dd>{v}</dd></div>)}</section>
<section class="component-narrative"><aside><span>Vicenza</span><strong>1967</strong><i></i><span>Lower price</span></aside><div class="component-copy"><p class="dropcap">In the 1960s Campagnolo represented the finest equipment on European racing bicycles. Beneath that market were far more everyday and affordable sports bicycles.</p><p><em>Valentino Super</em> appeared in catalogue 15 in 1967. <em>Valentino Extra</em> followed in 1968 and remained in catalogues through the 1970s and into the 1980s.</p><blockquote>The winged logo reached further down the bicycle shop’s price range.</blockquote><h2>Cheaper — but not merely tin</h2><p>The construction mixed formed metal, cast parts and a plate cage around the pulley wheels. Less refined than Nuovo Record, it was not simply thin stamped plates.</p><p>Friction shifting demanded careful adjustment. Greater play and lower stiffness made precise changes harder than with Campagnolo’s expensive models.</p><h2>A front derailleur from an older world</h2><p>Model 2050 used an external arm to move the mechanism sideways, a design rooted in Campagnolo’s early front derailleurs.</p><p>On the pictured bicycle it accompanies another maker’s chainset, typical of the mixed equipment often found at this level.</p></div></section>
<section class="catalog-spread component-photo-pair"><figure><img src={`${images}/frontskifter.jpg`} alt="Valentino front derailleur" loading="lazy"/><figcaption>The external arm and engraved logo. Photo: The Evolution of Bikes.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src={`${images}/bagskifter.jpg`} alt="Valentino Extra rear derailleur detail" loading="lazy"/><figcaption>Open construction, return spring and pulley cage.</figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="friction-section"><div><p class="section-label">“The bad and the ugly”?</p><h2>A later verdict, not a period name</h2></div><div><p>Collectors and mechanics sometimes use this harsh description, but there is no basis for treating it as a common contemporary nickname.</p><p>Valentino was not intended to beat Record in weight, finish or precision. It gave an affordable bicycle serviceable Campagnolo shifting.</p></div></section>
<section class="component-closing"><p class="section-label">What the component tells us</p><blockquote>Valentino matters precisely because it was not Campagnolo’s best.</blockquote><p>It records the marque’s attempt to move beyond the top of the peloton and into a larger market.</p></section>
<section class="period-sources"><p class="section-label">Sources and image notes</p><div><p>Names and dates were checked against Campagnolo catalogues; judgements about shifting quality are kept separate.</p><ul><li><a href="https://www.campyonly.com/history/catalogs/catalog_15.pdf">Campagnolo catalogue 15 (1967)</a></li><li><a href="https://www.disraeligears.co.uk/site/campagnolo_-_catalogues_newsletters.html">Disraeli Gears catalogue index</a></li><li><a href="https://www.campyonly.com/history/catalogs/catalog_17a.pdf">Campagnolo catalogue 17a (1975)</a></li></ul></div></section><nav class="era-bottom-nav"><a href={`${base}/en/components/`}>← All components</a><a href={`${base}/en/periods/1970-1979/`}>Read about 1970–1979 →</a></nav></article></main><EnglishFooter/></body></html>
